WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.594
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.894 --> 00:00:09.362
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.662 --> 00:00:16.052
Unit twenty-eight: Open Science. Today you will report widely held views with passive reporting verbs.

4
00:00:16.352 --> 00:00:18.865
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.021
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:25.210
Open access.

7
00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:30.817
Open access means free, unrestricted online access to research.

8
00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:34.717
Open access removes paywalls for readers worldwide.

9
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:38.329
Your turn. Say: Open access.

10
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.551
Open access.

11
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:44.724
Transparency.

12
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:49.585
Transparency means openness about methods, data and decisions.

13
00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:52.752
Transparency makes errors easier to detect.

14
00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:56.165
Your turn. Say: Transparency.

15
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.315
Transparency.

16
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.167
Replication.

17
00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:07.133
Replication means repeating a study to check whether results hold.

18
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.458
Many famous findings have failed replication.

19
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:13.993
Your turn. Say: Replication.

20
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:17.927
Replication.

21
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:19.754
Data sharing.

22
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.189
Data sharing means making research data available to others.

23
00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:27.003
Data sharing accelerates discovery.

24
00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:30.362
Your turn. Say: Data sharing.

25
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.503
Data sharing.

26
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.181
Preprint.

27
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:40.506
Preprint means a version of a paper shared before peer review.

28
00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:43.824
Preprints spread rapidly during the pandemic.

29
00:01:44.417 --> 00:01:47.034
Your turn. Say: Preprint.

30
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:50.739
Preprint.

31
00:01:51.483 --> 00:01:52.868
Reproducibility.

32
00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:58.019
Reproducibility means the ability to obtain the same results from the same data.

33
00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:00.895
Shared code improves reproducibility.

34
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:04.797
Your turn. Say: Reproducibility.

35
00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:08.948
Reproducibility.

36
00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:10.847
Collaboration.

37
00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:15.614
Collaboration means working together across institutions and borders.

38
00:02:16.217 --> 00:02:19.029
Open science encourages global collaboration.

39
00:02:19.617 --> 00:02:22.513
Your turn. Say: Collaboration.

40
00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:26.623
Collaboration.

41
00:02:27.383 --> 00:02:28.141
Public.

42
00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.985
Public means ordinary people in society as a whole.

43
00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:35.651
Publicly funded research should be available to the public.

44
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.450
Your turn. Say: Public.

45
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.197
Public.

46
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.022
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.883 --> 00:02:51.833
Visal, why are so many scientists now sharing their data openly?

48
00:02:52.950 --> 00:03:01.814
It is widely argued that open data improves reproducibility. Others can check whether the analysis holds up.

49
00:03:02.917 --> 00:03:04.208
Aren't there risks?

50
00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:14.204
Certainly. It has been suggested that sensitive data could be misused, so participants' privacy must be protected.

51
00:03:15.317 --> 00:03:18.352
And preprints? Aren't they unreliable?

52
00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:28.683
They are thought to speed up discovery, but it is generally accepted that readers should treat them cautiously until they are peer-reviewed.

53
00:03:29.833 --> 00:03:31.636
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:32.483 --> 00:03:36.178
Grammar focus: Passive reporting verbs (It is argued that).

55
00:03:36.478 --> 00:03:37.618
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.317 --> 00:03:41.826
It is widely argued that open data improves reproducibility.

57
00:03:42.717 --> 00:03:45.912
It has been suggested that sensitive data could be misused.

58
00:03:46.817 --> 00:03:49.165
Preprints are thought to speed up discovery.

59
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:58.636
Passive reporting verbs present views without naming who holds them: 'It is argued that …' or 'X is thought to …'. They create an objective, academic distance.

60
00:03:59.383 --> 00:04:03.963
Be careful! Don't say: It is widely argue that open data helps.

61
00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.409
Say: It is widely argued that open data helps.

62
00:04:09.709 --> 00:04:13.055
Use the past participle: 'It is argued that …'.

63
00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:16.693
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.174
Science Without Walls.

65
00:04:19.474 --> 00:04:24.980
Traditionally, research was published in expensive journals, accessible mainly to wealthy universities.

66
00:04:25.967 --> 00:04:31.124
The open science movement seeks to change this through open access, data sharing and preprints.

67
00:04:32.133 --> 00:04:38.823
It is widely argued that open data improves reproducibility, since independent researchers can verify published results.

68
00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:46.105
It has also been suggested that transparency discourages misconduct, as errors become far easier to detect.

69
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:53.929
Critics, however, warn that openness must be balanced against participants' privacy and the risk of misinterpretation.

70
00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:59.070
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:59.917 --> 00:05:04.261
It is widely blank that open data improves reproducibility.

72
00:05:04.561 --> 00:05:08.226
A, argue. B, argued. C, arguing.

73
00:05:11.767 --> 00:05:13.720
The answer is B: argued.

74
00:05:14.883 --> 00:05:21.355
Transform: 'People think preprints speed up discovery.' → Preprints blank speed up discovery.

75
00:05:21.655 --> 00:05:25.912
A, are thought to. B, are thinking to. C, thought to be.

76
00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:31.942
The answer is A: are thought to.

77
00:05:33.083 --> 00:05:35.710
A paper shared before peer review is a blank.

78
00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:39.768
A, preprint. B, proceedings. C, replication.

79
00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:45.321
The answer is A: preprint.

80
00:05:46.483 --> 00:05:49.470
Obtaining the same results from the same data shows blank.

81
00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:54.364
A, reproducibility. B, open access. C, collaboration.

82
00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.596
The answer is A: reproducibility.

83
00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:05.549
According to the reading, what must openness be balanced against?

84
00:06:05.849 --> 00:06:11.000
A, participants' privacy. B, journal profits. C, faster discovery.

85
00:06:14.567 --> 00:06:17.727
The answer is A: participants' privacy.

86
00:06:18.933 --> 00:06:22.247
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:23.083 --> 00:06:26.523
What are the benefits and risks of sharing research data openly?

88
00:06:43.317 --> 00:06:44.805
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:45.105 --> 00:07:19.240
It is widely argued that open data makes research more trustworthy, because others can check and reproduce the results. It also allows scientists in less wealthy countries to build on existing work without expensive subscriptions. For example, shared genetic data helped vaccines to be developed remarkably quickly. However, it has been suggested that personal data could be misused if not properly anonymised. Overall, I believe the benefits outweigh the risks, provided privacy is protected.

90
00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:21.238
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:27.287
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive reporting verbs (it is argued that).

92
00:07:27.587 --> 00:07:31.423
Today's value is cooperation. Work together with friends.

93
00:07:31.723 --> 00:07:34.492
See you in Unit twenty-nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:34.792 --> 00:07:38.745
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

